issue_context.body:
(Updated with more general description, following comment by @brunnerh )

### Describe the bug

Inside an `#each` block, when a value is assigned to a deeply nested prop value, all intermediate props are skipped.

Trying to assign a value to `person.a.really.long.path.to.first` is compiled into setting the value of `person.first` instead.

This means that for code like this:

```html
<script>
	let people = $state([{name:{first:'rob'}}]);	
</script>

{#each people as person}
	<button on:click={()=>{
		person.name.first = "dave"; //person.name.first = "dave";
		person.first = "dave"; //person.first = "dave";
		person.a.really.long.path.to.first = "dave"; //person.a.really.long.path.to.first = "dave";
		people = people; //people = people;
	}}>person.name.first = "dave"</button>
{/each}

<h3>JSON output</h3>
{#each people as person}
	<div>{JSON.stringify(people)}</div>
{/each}
```
The `on:click` function is compiled to:
```js
var on_click = (_, person, people) => {
	$.unwrap(person).first = "dave"; //person.name.first = "dave";
	$.unwrap(person).first = "dave"; //person.first = "dave";
	$.unwrap(person).first = "dave"; //person.a.really.long.path.to.first = "dave";
	$.set(people, $.get(people)); //people = people;
};
```
If `person[i].name.first` is used the bug does not occur.

Also, if a temporary value is used like this, the bug does not occur:
```
let temp = person;
temp.name.first = "dave";
temp.first = "dave";
temp.a.really.long.path.to.first = "dave";
people = people;
```

